WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.346
Scenario: A QR code on a delivery notice opens

2
00:00:03.346 --> 00:00:06.692
a page asking you to download an Android package

3
00:00:06.692 --> 00:00:10.038
file directly because the “official app store is unavailable.”

4
00:00:10.038 --> 00:00:13.385
Safest response: Option A: Do not sideload it; verify

5
00:00:13.385 --> 00:00:17.474
the delivery through the courier’s official app or independently located website.

6
00:00:17.474 --> 00:00:21.564
Use an authenticated distribution channel and independently verify the delivery request.

7
00:00:21.564 --> 00:00:24.910
Option B is unsafe because The printed notice does

8
00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:28.628
not authenticate the source or integrity of the installation file.

9
00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:31.974
Option C is unsafe because This transfers risk to

10
00:00:31.974 --> 00:00:35.321
another person and may propagate malware. Option D is

11
00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:38.667
unsafe because One permission choice does not make an

12
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:42.013
untrusted package safe; it may request more later or

13
00:00:42.013 --> 00:00:45.359
contain malicious code. In practice: Close the page, do

14
00:00:45.359 --> 00:00:48.705
not install the package, and verify shipment status through

15
00:00:48.705 --> 00:00:52.051
the courier’s independently accessed official service. Key takeaway: Do

16
00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:55.397
not install an unsolicited app package from a QR-linked

17
00:00:55.397 --> 00:00:58.000
page; use a trusted app distribution channel.
